\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Experimental sensitivity comparison between traditional binned GLS periodograms and Neural Simulation-Based Inference (NSBI) on LHC dimuon spectra. Left: Unbinned profile likelihood test statistic $q(\varepsilon ) = -2\Delta \ln \mathcal  {L}$ for multiple dilation factors $\lambda \in [1.5, 5.0]$, identifying the 95\% CL threshold ($q=3.841$). Right: 95\% CL upper limits demonstrating a consistent $\mathbf  {9.5\times }$ sensitivity multiplier achieved by unbinned NSBI across the full scaling spectrum, effectively breaking the zeptouniverse sensitivity barrier.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:nsbi_comparison}{{1}{3}{Experimental sensitivity comparison between traditional binned GLS periodograms and Neural Simulation-Based Inference (NSBI) on LHC dimuon spectra. Left: Unbinned profile likelihood test statistic $q(\varepsilon ) = -2\Delta \ln \mathcal {L}$ for multiple dilation factors $\lambda \in [1.5, 5.0]$, identifying the 95\% CL threshold ($q=3.841$). Right: 95\% CL upper limits demonstrating a consistent $\mathbf {9.5\times }$ sensitivity multiplier achieved by unbinned NSBI across the full scaling spectrum, effectively breaking the zeptouniverse sensitivity barrier}{figure.1}{}}
